{"cuisine/california-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/california-cuisine","n":"California cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["California cuisine is a food movement that originated in Northern California, in the United States. The cuisine focuses on dishes that use local and sustainable ingredients, with attention to seasonality."],["The food is historically chef-driven; Alice Waters's restaurant Chez Panisse is an iconic example. Dishes and meals low in saturated fats and high in fresh vegetables and fruits with lean meats and seafood from the California coast often define the style."],["The term \"California cuisine\" arose as a result of culinary movements in the last decades of the 20th century and is not to be confused with the traditional foods of California. California fusion cuisine has been influenced by French, American, Italian, Mexican, and Chinese culinary styles, among other food cultures."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"california-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Sushi bake","dish/sushi-bake"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Chez Panisse Menu Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Alice Waters, 1982","note":"Seasonal menus from the Berkeley restaurant central to California cuisine.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"Chez Panisse Café Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Alice Waters, 1999","note":"Simpler ingredient-led dishes from the Chez Panisse upstairs café.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780060175832","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780060175832"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/b/b4/Chez_Panisse_pizza.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"stu_spivack","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chez_Panisse_pizza.jpg"}},"cuisine/korean-temple-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/korean-temple-cuisine","n":"Korean temple cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"],["Buddhist cuisine","cuisine/buddhist-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in South Korea","intro":[["Korean temple cuisine refers to a type of cuisine that originated in Buddhist temples of Korea. Since Buddhism was introduced into Korea, Buddhist traditions have strongly influenced Korean cuisine as well. During the Silla period (57 BC – 935 AD), chalbap (찰밥, a bowl of cooked glutinous rice) yakgwa (약과, a fried dessert) and yumilgwa (a fried and puffed rice snack) were served for Buddhist altars and have been developed into types of hangwa, Korean traditional confectionery. During the Goryeo period, sangchu ssam (wraps made with lettuce), yaksik, and yakgwa were developed, so spread to China and other countries. Since the Joseon Dynasty, Buddhist cuisine has been established in Korea according to regions and temples."],["On the other hand, royal court cuisine is closely related to Korean temple cuisine. In the past, when the royal court maids called sanggung, who were assigned to Suragan, where they prepared the king's meals, became old, they had to leave the royal palace. Therefore, many of them entered Buddhist temples to become nuns. As the result, culinary techniques and recipes of the royal cuisine were integrated into Buddhist cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"korean-temple-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_temple_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"],["Buddhist cuisine","cuisine/buddhist-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Korean Temple Cooking: Lessons on Life and Buddhism, with Recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Hoo Nam Seelmann","note":"Recipes and teachings of the Buddhist nun Jeong Kwan, known from Chef's Table.","url":"https://www.hardiegrantgift.com.au/books/korean-temple-cooking-9781964786186","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781964786186"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"],["Buddhist cuisine","cuisine/buddhist-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/2/2a/Korea-Seoul-Insadong-Sanchon-02.jpg/500px-Korea-Seoul-Insadong-Sanchon-02.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Julie","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Seoul-Insadong-Sanchon-02.jpg"}},"cuisine/miao-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/miao-cuisine","n":"Miao cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Miao cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"miao-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miao_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Miao cooking","count":1,"links":[["Hmong cuisine","cuisine/hmong-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/sarawakian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sarawakian-cuisine","n":"Sarawakian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Malaysia","intro":[["Sarawakian cuisine is a regional cuisine of Malaysia. Similar to the rest of Malaysian cuisine, Sarawak food is based on staples such as rice, while other ingredients and food preparations distinct from that of Peninsular Malaysia come from the influence of the state's varied geography, multi-ethnic populations and indigenous cultures. Sarawak cuisine is less spicy and more subtle in taste than elsewhere in Malaysia. It uses fresh seafood and natural herbs like turmeric, lemongrass, ginger, lime and tapioca leaves."],["Each ethnic group has their own specialty dishes. Among the Iban, popular foods include tubu (stems), tuak (alcoholic beverage made from rice wine) and pansuh (dish cooked with bamboo). The Malay have bubur pedas (porridge) and kek lapis Sarawak (Sarawak layer cake); the Bidayuh have asam siok (chicken rice cooked in bamboo) and sup ponas Bidayuh (soup dish made of tapioca). The Melanau make tebaloi (Sago palm crackers), sagu (extracted from Sago palm) and umai (raw fish mixed with lime juice) and the Orang Ulu are known for garam barrio (Highlands salt), kikid (broth), tengayen (local young leaves), and urum giruq (pudding)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"sarawakian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarawakian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Guai Shu Shu (Sarawakian cuisines)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"guaishushu1.com","note":"Sarawak-born home cook Kenneth Goh's blog, with a compilation of Sarawakian dishes.","url":"https://www.guaishushu1.com/special-compilation-of-sarawakian-cuisines/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"House of Annie","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"houseofannie.com","note":"Food blog by a couple with Sarawak roots, known for Sarawakian recipes.","url":"https://houseofannie.com/category/recipes/page/4","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Tamu: A Guest at the Bornean Table","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Bryan Koh","note":"Recipes shared by families across Borneo, not this region alone.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/6c/Laksa_Sarawak.JPG/500px-Laksa_Sarawak.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Jhlam79","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laksa_Sarawak.JPG"}},"cuisine/sami-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sami-cuisine","n":"Sami cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Sámi cuisine is the cuisine of peoples from the Sápmi territory of the Sámi people, which spans Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia. Its traditional cuisine of each area has individual traits."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"sami-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sami_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Taste of Sápmi: Sámi Cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Victoria Harnesk","note":"Sámi recipes and food traditions, including reindeer and foraged ingredients.","url":"","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/d/d6/Poronk%C3%A4ristys.jpg/500px-Poronk%C3%A4ristys.jpg","w":500,"h":400,"by":"Htm","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poronk%C3%A4ristys.jpg"}},"ingredient/wheat-flour":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/wheat-flour","n":"Wheat flour","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 187 dishes across 108 cuisines","intro":[["Wheat flour is a powder made from the grinding of common wheat used for human consumption. Wheat varieties are called \"soft\" or \"weak\" if gluten content is low, and are called \"hard\" or \"strong\" if they have high gluten content. Hard flour, or bread flour, is high in gluten, with 12% to 14% gluten content, and its dough has elastic toughness that holds its shape well once baked. Soft flour is comparatively low in gluten and thus results in a loaf with a finer, crumbly texture. Soft flour, in the US, is usually divided into cake flour, which is the lowest in gluten, and pastry flour, which has slightly more gluten than cake flour."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-wheat-flour"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat_flour"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":108,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Jalangkote","dish/jalangkote"],["Klappertaart","dish/klappertaart"],["Kue","dish/kue"],["Kue ape","dish/kue-ape"],["Kue leker","dish/kue-leker"],["Kue pukis","dish/kue-pukis"],["Kue putri salju","dish/kue-putri-salju"],["Kue semprong","dish/kue-semprong"],["Kue talam","dish/kue-talam"],["Meuseukat","dish/meuseukat"],["Mie aceh","dish/mie-aceh"],["Pempek","dish/pempek"],["Roti buaya","dish/roti-buaya"],["Serabi","dish/serabi"],["Timphan","dish/timphan"]]],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Battenberg cake","dish/battenberg-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Crumble","dish/crumble"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"],["Rich tea","dish/rich-tea"],["Spotted dick","dish/spotted-dick"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Baba (cake)","dish/baba-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Goulash","dish/goulash"],["Gugelhupf","dish/gugelhupf"],["Karelian pasty","dish/karelian-pasty"],["Kolach (bread)","dish/kolach-bread"],["Kringl